@chapter wp Workspace
@cindex wp
@cindex workpapers
@cindex print arrays

This is a workspace for printing arrays (on paper). It offers control
over many aspects of the printed page.

The workspace generates an HTML page which can then be loaded into
one's browser and printed.

@menu

* Howto ::              Create a report, basic instructions.
* Init ::               Create a blank report.
* Set data ::           Add an array of data.
* Attributes ::         Data must have attributes to describe formatting.
* Stylesheet ::         A style sheet describes formatting for a class of data.
* Required Classes ::   Parts of the report that require their own classes.
* Other Tweaks ::       Other things to try.
* Distances ::          Units of measure in a class.
@end menu

@node Howto
@section Howto

Reports are a complex data structure initiated and maintained through
functions in the wp workspace.

A report consists of an array, report heading, and stylesheet. For
each cell in the array additional attributes can be saved for
formatting the report cell by cell.

The basic process is:

@itemize @bullet

@item Create the report with wp∆init 'Report Name'

@item Add the array to print with wp∆setData

@item Add attributes to cells in the array. Attributes identify the format
strings for numbers and the applicable stylesheet classes.

@item Create or modify a stylesheet.

@item Generate the HTML for the report with wp∆html∆assemble.

@end itemize

@node Init
@section Initiating the report

Use the function wp∆init to create a report.  Init requires a short
character string as its sole argument and will print that string in
the report as an identifier for the report.

The report itself is a lexicon of Id, Data, Attributes, Stylesheet,
Head_ln01 through Head_ln03, and Author. wp∆init will use the
environment's USER for the Author.


@node Set data
@section Adding data to your report

By default, a report's data is a single cell of 0.

wp requires an array of rank 2 and expects that descriptions of the
rows and columns will be included in the array.  There is no enforced
limit on the size of the array.  However, the target browser that
displays the array may limit what you can put on the screen.

The HTML coding is designed to allow the browser to determine column
widths, text wrapping within a cell and other features. As the browser
window size is changed the browser should reformat the report.

Users experienced in cascading style sheets can add style sheet classes
to establish actual widths, heights and placements for a more fixed
presentation @pxref{Stylesheet}.

@node Attributes
@section Attributes

An attribute is a name--value pair.  Each cell can have a list of
attributes. Two are in general use 'format' and 'class'.  Other
attributes will be passed to your browser as attributes of the
cell. For example a cell has three attributes, format, class and
passed. the coded HTML would be:

@example '<tr>...
         <td class=''number'' passed=''user-defined''>',
         CF14.2 fmt cell_amount,
         '</td>...</tr>'
@end example

The format attribute is used by the workspace to format the number of
the cell. APL2's format by example ('55,550.00' ⍕ number) and function
fmt @pxref{ fmt} can be used.

The class attribute selects the style sheet class to use for the cell
@pxref{Stylesheet}.

Accessor functions for attributes have a prefix wp∆attr∆. They are:

@deffn ∇ report wp∆attr∆getCellAttr row_number column_number
Function returns a lexicon (@pxref{lex}) of attributes.
@end deffn

@deffn ∇ report wp∆attr∆setCellAttr row_number Column_number lexicon
Function adds, or replaces the name value pair.  The function expects
a lexicon of name value pairs. For example:

@example
rpt←rpt wp∆attr∆setCellAttr 15 3,⊂lex∆from_alist 'format' 'CF10.2' 'class' 'number'
@end example

@end deffn

@deffn ∇ report wp∆attr∆getQuadAttr start_row start_column end_row end_column
Function returns a portion of the Attribute array.
@end deffn

@deffn ∇ report wp∆attr∆setQuadAttr start_row start_column end_row end_column encapsulated_lexicon(s)
Funtion sets a rectangular portion of the Attribute array. If one
lexicon is given that lexicon will be copied to each cell in the
rectangle. If more than one lexicon is given, the size and shape of
the final argument must match the size and shape of the rectangle.
@end deffn

@node Stylesheet
@section Stylesheet

The style sheet is a lexicon of classes that describe how to present a
cell in the data.  Each class within the sheet is a lexicon of
properties. An example style sheet is returned by the function
wp∆defaultSS.

Properties are defined in the global variable wp∆ssp∆propertyList
which is derived from the W3.com cascading style sheet standards. The
standards define what values each property may take.

@itemize

@item Create a style sheet wp∆ss∆init or wp∆defaultSS.

@item Create classes with wp∆ssc∆init

@item Add properties to the class.

@item Add classes to the spread sheet.

@item Assign the spreadsheet to the report

@end itemize
  
@deffn ∇ wp∆ss∆init
Function returns an empty style sheet.
@end deffn

@deffn ∇ wp∆defaultSS
Function returns a style sheet with classes defined for a typical
array.
@end deffn

@deffn ∇ wp∆ssc∆init
Function returns a new style sheet class.
@end deffn

@deffn class wp∆ssc∆assignProp Property
Function adds a property to a class. Properties are name--value
pairs. For instance:

@example
  r4←wp∆ssr∆init '.page-head'
  r4←r4 wp∆ssr∆assignProp 'font-size' 'large'
@end example

@end deffn

@deffn ∇ StyleSheet wp∆ss∆assign Class

Assign a class to a stylesheet.  Function will silently replace an
existing class.

@example
  ss←ss wp∆ss∆assignClass r4
@end example

@end deffn

@node Required Classes
@section Required Classes

Parts of the final report require their own class.

@itemize

@item Body is a global class that applies to whole report.

@item colhead describes how to format the descriptions at the top of
each column.

@item number describes the formatting for any number in your array.

@item page-head describes how the head lines will appear.

@item initial block is for the report ID, author and creation date
that appear in a distinctive block.

@end itemize

All of these classes are included in the default style sheet. You may
not like the results if they are omitted from the published style
sheet.

@node Other Tweaks
@section Other Tweaks

@itemize

@item Initials.  Use 'wp∆setInitialFlag 0' to remove the initial block
from the report.

@end itemize

@node Distances
@section Distances

Many properties are looking for a distance.  Distances can be fixed,
relative or percentages (a number followed by %). There should be no
white space between the amount the the measure.

Fixed distances can be measured in:

@itemize

@item pt (points) or 1/72 of an inch. Fonts are measured in points.

@item pc (picas) or 12pt

@item mm (millimeters)

@item cm (centimeters)

@item in (inches)

@end itemize

Relative units are determined by the font:

@itemize

@item em is the width of the capital M.  This should be the point size of
the font and the distance between baselines.

@item ex is the height of the letter x.  This should be the base line and
the mean line of lower case letters.

@item
px (pixels). This is more of a measure for monitors than printers.

@quotation
You may be thinking that because elements on web pages are measured in
numbers of pixels, and because pixels are considered a relative
measurement, that they are the answer to all font size problems. It
would be nice if the were.  For some designers, control over text at
the pixel level is intoxicating.

@author Jennifer Niederst Robbins, Web Design in a Nutshell, O'Reilly

@end quotation

@end itemize

